# The Workoho global gitignore.
#
#   git config --global core.excludesfile ~/.gitignore_global
#
# Only what this machine and its tools produce, never what a project produces.
# A pattern here works for you and for nobody else, so anything a repository
# needs ignored belongs in that repository's own .gitignore -- otherwise it
# looks handled here and lands in the next person's working tree.
#
# The operating system and editor sections below are from gitignore.io
# (toptal.com/developers/gitignore); the language and framework sections it
# also offers are deliberately not here, for the reason above.

# --- macOS -----------------------------------------------------
# General
.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride
# The macOS custom-folder-icon file is deliberately absent. Its real name is
# "Icon" plus a carriage return, which a plain `Icon` line does not match, and
# every pattern that does -- `Icon?`, `Icon*` -- also matches Icons/ and Icon1
# and would hide them on every machine. It only appears when somebody sets a
# custom folder icon; ignore it in that repository if it ever shows up.
# Thumbnails
._*
# Files that might appear in the root of a volume
.DocumentRevisions-V100
.fseventsd
.Spotlight-V100
.TemporaryItems
.Trashes
.VolumeIcon.icns
.com.apple.timemachine.donotpresent
# Directories potentially created on remote AFP share
.AppleDB
.AppleDesktop
Network Trash Folder
Temporary Items
.apdisk

# --- macOS Patch -----------------------------------------------
# iCloud generated files
*.icloud

# --- Windows ---------------------------------------------------
# Windows thumbnail cache files
Thumbs.db
Thumbs.db:encryptable
ehthumbs.db
ehthumbs_vista.db
# Dump file
*.stackdump
# Folder config file
[Dd]esktop.ini
# Recycle Bin used on file shares
$RECYCLE.BIN/
# Windows shortcuts, which the shell creates
*.lnk

# --- Linux -----------------------------------------------------
*~
# temporary files which can be created if a process still has a handle open of a deleted file
.fuse_hidden*
# KDE directory preferences
.directory
# Linux trash folder which might appear on any partition or disk
.Trash-*
# .nfs files are created when an open file is removed but is still being accessed
.nfs*

# --- Git -------------------------------------------------------
# Created by git for backups. To disable backups in Git:
# $ git config --global mergetool.keepBackup false
*.orig
# Created by git when using merge tools for conflicts
*.BACKUP.*
*.BASE.*
*.LOCAL.*
*.REMOTE.*
*_BACKUP_*.txt
*_BASE_*.txt
*_LOCAL_*.txt
*_REMOTE_*.txt

# --- direnv ----------------------------------------------------
# The evaluated cache. .envrc itself is not here: it belongs to a project
# and is usually committed.
.direnv


# --- Secrets, as a second net -----------------------------------
# The deliberate exception to "nothing a project produces". Everything else
# here is scoped to the machine because a global pattern hides a repository's
# omission from the person who made it -- but the damage is not symmetric. An
# unignored venv/ is somebody committing clutter; an unignored .env.local is a
# published secret, and that one cannot be taken back.
#
# .env stays tracked: under the cascade repo-setup describes it carries values
# that are harmless after a fork. Only the .local files are secret, and
# .env.local.example is a template that has to stay visible -- the patterns
# below leave it alone, which was verified rather than assumed.
.env.local
.env.*.local

# --- Agent and editor state, per machine ------------------------
# Claude Code's per-person permissions and settings. The repository's own
# .gitignore carries this too, on purpose: this line only protects this
# machine, and a file that is untracked only here is a trap for everyone else.
**/.claude/settings.local.json

# No Codex equivalent exists, which is worth stating so nobody goes looking:
# its project layer is .codex/config.toml and that is meant to be committed.
# Tools nobody here uses are deliberately absent as well -- a pattern for an
# editor or agent that is not on the workstation list is noise in a file whose
# whole value is that every line earns its place.

# Editor and IDE state that is a person's rather than a project's. .vscode/ is
# deliberately absent -- a repository often commits it to share recommended
# extensions and launch configurations, and ignoring it globally hides that.
# .idea/ is absent for the same reason: code styles, inspection profiles and
# shared run configurations live there and are meant to be committed. Only the
# per-user members below are ignored.
.idea/workspace.xml
.idea/tasks.xml
.idea/usage.statistics.xml
.idea/shelf/
.idea/httpRequests/
*.iml
*.swp
*.swo
.*.sw?

# Four things the generator offered and this file refuses, because each hides
# something a repository is supposed to carry:
#
#   .env    -- committed under the .env cascade repo-setup describes, holding
#              the values nobody should have to look up. Ignoring it here means
#              nobody can add it without git add -f, on every machine with this
#              file.
#   .envrc  -- direnv's own configuration, which belongs to the project.
#   *.zip, *.dll -- offered under "PowerShell", and project artifacts: a test
#              fixture or a vendored assembly would vanish from git status with
#              no indication why.
#   *.msi, *.msix, *.cab, *.msm, *.msp -- offered under "Windows", and build
#              output rather than anything Windows leaves lying around. A
#              repository that produces or tests an installer would lose it.
#
# The pattern in all four: a section named after a platform or a tool still
# lists what that tool builds. Read the entries, not the heading.
